<article class="env-definition" id="03XG">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03XG">Definition <span data-tag="03XG">6.2.7.4</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. A <em>class of short morphisms for $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$</em> is a collection $S$ of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ with the following properties: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
Every identity morphism of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ belongs to $S$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every $2$-simplex 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ &amp;  Y \ar [dr]^{f'} &amp;  \\ X \ar [ur]^{f''} \ar [rr]^{f} &amp;  &amp;  Z } \]
</div>
<p>
 of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, if $f$ and $f'$ belong to $S$, then $f''$ also belongs to $S$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(3)$</dt>
<dd><p>
Every morphism $f: X \rightarrow Z$ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ admits an $S$-optimal factorization (Definition <a data-tag="03XC" href="/tag/03XC">6.2.7.1</a>). 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(4)$</dt>
<dd><p>
Every morphism of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ can be obtained as a composition of morphisms which belong to $S$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
